    "title": "Candidates trade barbs on opioids and taxes in NJ debate - NewsBreak",
    "text": "By Eileen Lehpamer ,\n2 hours ago\nNEW BRUNSWICK, N.J. (PIX11) — Some fiery moments during the second New Jersey Gubernatorial Debate Wednesday night at the New Brunswick Performing Arts Center.\nAt one point, both Republican Jack Ciattarelli and Democrat Mikie Sherrill said “shame on you” to the other.\nMore Local News Ciattarelli again demanded that Sherrill release her full disciplinary record from her time at the U.S. Naval Academy after it was revealed that she didn’t “walk” at her graduation. She says it was because she wouldn’t report classmates involved in a cheating scandal.\nSherill accused Ciattarelli of working with opioid companies during the deadly opioid crisis.\nSherrill said Ciattarelli “got paid to develop an app so that more people can get more opioids and die.” Ciattarelli responded twice with: “I got to walk at my college graduation”. Sherrill snapped back, “And I’m so glad that you then went on to kill tens of thousands of people in New Jersey, including children.”\nAt a news conference after the debate, Ciattarelli was asked to respond to the accusations. He said:\n“Another desperate tactic, by a desperate campaign on behalf of a desperate candidate, it’s a lie.”\nThe candidates also spoke about property taxes, energy costs, the role of the national guard, and free speech. At one point, both candidates agreed that New Jersey should remain a state where self-serve gas is prohibited. Ciattarelli, light-heartedly stating, “Jersey girls don’t like to pump their own gas.”\nCopyright 2025 Nexstar Media, Inc. All rights reserved. This material may not be published, broadcast, rewritten, or redistributed. For the latest news, weather, sports, and streaming video, head to PIX11.",
